self_contained_problem:
Consider a low-power wide-area wireless network operating in sub-GHz unlicensed bands using a chirp-spread-spectrum modulation (LoRa-style physical layer), in which end devices transmit to gateways across heterogeneous deployment environments. Let L denote a wireless link between a transmitter at position p_t and a receiver at position p_r, embedded in an environment E that may include open-air regions, line-of-sight (LOS) and non-line-of-sight (NLOS) segments, concrete or steel obstructions, indoor settings (e.g., homes, hospitals, schools), and vegetated areas (e.g., forests). Assume each link experiences a combination of large-scale path loss, small-scale fading, shadowing, and direction-dependent (anisotropic) attenuation due to dynamic and static obstacles that vary with bearing from the transmitter.

Develop a mathematical framework that, given a description of the environment E (e.g., a map of obstacle classes and their electromagnetic properties along the propagation path) and link parameters (carrier frequency, spreading factor, bandwidth, transmit power, antenna heights), provides:

(a) a closed-form or computable model L(p_t, p_r, E) for the expected link quality (e.g., received signal strength, signal-to-noise ratio, and packet reception ratio), explicitly accounting for direction-dependent path loss, multi-medium attenuation, and fading in transitional regions where link quality is temporally unstable;

(b) a coverage-prediction system that, from such a model, outputs the spatial coverage region of a gateway at p_t under environment E, capturing anisotropy and the existence of transitional links;

(c) provable accuracy or error bounds on link-quality estimates and coverage predictions across the listed deployment environments, without requiring extensive on-site measurements.

The problem is to construct such a model and system, or to prove fundamental limits on their achievable accuracy.

Setup. Let (X,d) be a metric space without isolated points. A non-autonomous dynamical system (NADS) on X is a pair (X,f_{1,∞}) where f_{1,∞}=(f_1,f_2,f_3,…) is a sequence of continuous self-maps f_i:X→X. For n≥1 set F_n = f_n ∘ f_{n-1} ∘ ⋯ ∘ f_1, and F_0 = id_X. The orbit of x∈X is orb(x,f_{1,∞}) = {F_n(x) : n≥0}.

Definitions.
• (X,f_{1,∞}) is topologically transitive if for every pair of nonempty open sets U,V⊂X there exists n≥1 with F_n(U)∩V ≠ ∅.
• A point x∈X is periodic for f_{1,∞} if there exists p≥1 with F_{kp}(x)=x for all k≥0; let Per(f_{1,∞}) denote the set of periodic points. We say "every point of X is periodic" if Per(f_{1,∞}) = X.
• A periodic point x is invariant if f_i(x)=x for some structure giving f_n(x) periodic in n; concretely, two invariant periodic points x,y are required to have disjoint orbits: orb(x,f_{1,∞}) ∩ orb(y,f_{1,∞}) = ∅.
• (X,f_{1,∞}) is finitely generated if the set {f_i : i≥1} of maps appearing in the sequence is finite; otherwise it is not finitely generated.
• (X,f_{1,∞}) is sensitive if there exists ε>0 such that for every x∈X and every neighborhood U of x there exist y∈U and n≥0 with d(F_n(x),F_n(y)) > ε.

Question. Does there exist a non-autonomous dynamical system (X,f_{1,∞}) on a metric space X without isolated points satisfying all of the following?
(1) f_{1,∞} is topologically transitive;
(2) Per(f_{1,∞}) = X;
(3) there exist two invariant periodic points x,y∈X with orb(x,f_{1,∞}) ∩ orb(y,f_{1,∞}) = ∅;
(4) (X,f_{1,∞}) is not finitely generated;
(5) (X,f_{1,∞}) is not sensitive.

Consider a hierarchy of temporal contact networks indexed by population scales s = 1, 2, ..., S (for instance, household, workplace/school, city, country, global). At each scale s, let G_s(t) = (V_s, E_s(t)) be a time-varying graph on a vertex set V_s, with the edge set E_s(t) evolving according to a prescribed stochastic or deterministic rule with characteristic time scale τ_s. Couple these scales by specifying inclusion/aggregation maps π_{s+1,s}: V_s → V_{s+1} (so that nodes at a finer scale are grouped into nodes at a coarser scale) together with cross-scale interaction rules that allow contacts realized at one scale to influence the edge dynamics and the spread of infection at other scales. On this coupled multi-scale temporal network, run a compartmental epidemic process (for example SIS or SIR) with per-contact transmission rate β and recovery rate γ.

Develop a mathematical framework that:
(a) defines a meaningful coupled multi-scale temporal contact network model in which the temporal dynamics at every scale are non-trivial and interact;
(b) characterizes the epidemic threshold (the critical value of β/γ above which a macroscopic outbreak occurs) as a function of the temporal statistics and topologies of the networks at each scale and of the cross-scale coupling;
(c) identifies the qualitatively new epidemic and network-structural phenomena (e.g., metastability, scale-dependent thresholds, emergent bursts, localization, or synchronization of activity) that arise from coupling temporal networks across scales, and that are absent when each scale is treated as a closed, isolated population.

In particular, determine under what conditions the multi-scale epidemic threshold is determined by a single dominant scale versus by a genuinely multi-scale combination, and quantify the corrections to single-scale threshold formulas induced by the coupling.

Consider a federated learning protocol in which a server aggregates model updates contributed by a (potentially large and dynamic) population of client devices. To protect the contents of individual client updates during aggregation, the protocol uses a homomorphic encryption (HE) scheme: each client encrypts its update under a public key, the server homomorphically combines the ciphertexts (e.g., by adding them), and the resulting ciphertext is decrypted to reveal only the aggregate.

Assume the following realistic constraints on the federated setting:
(a) there is no permanently available, universally trusted external party that can be assumed not to collude with the server;
(b) clients are intermittently online, may drop out at any time, and the participating set changes from round to round;
(c) standard HE schemes used at scale (e.g., lattice-based schemes) require periodic refreshing/rotation of secret keys;
(d) any party that learns the secret key can decrypt individual ciphertexts and thus break privacy of single clients' updates;
(e) corrupted clients and a corrupted server may collude up to some bounded fraction.

Design and analyze a key-management and trust structure for the HE-based federated learning protocol that specifies (i) which entity or entities hold the decryption key material, (ii) how keys are generated, refreshed, and distributed across rounds with churning client participation, and (iii) a precise corruption/threshold model under which the protocol provably preserves the privacy of every honest client's individual update against the server and any colluding adversaries, without relying on a permanently trusted external decryptor.

Formally: produce a threshold (or otherwise distributed) HE-based federated aggregation protocol together with a security definition and proof, or establish an impossibility result, characterizing the trust assumptions necessary and sufficient to achieve client-level privacy of updates in this setting.

Consider a federated-learning style setting in which a single server interacts with $n$ clients over multiple rounds. In each round $t$, every client $i \in \{1,\dots,n\}$ holds a private input vector $x_i^{(t)} \in \mathbb{R}^d$ (typically high-dimensional and possibly sparse), and the server wishes to compute the aggregate $S^{(t)} = \sum_{i=1}^n x_i^{(t)}$ without learning anything else about the individual inputs. A secure aggregation protocol is a multi-party cryptographic protocol that delivers $S^{(t)}$ to the server while protecting the confidentiality of the $x_i^{(t)}$.

Existing constructions (e.g., based on pairwise masking with a public-key infrastructure setup) suffer from the following limitations:

(a) Trust assumption on the server. The server is assumed to behave semi-honestly (follow the protocol) at least during the public-key infrastructure / setup phase.

(b) Leakage from per-round aggregates. The server learns the exact per-round sum $S^{(t)}$, which may itself leak information about individual clients across rounds.

(c) Inefficiency for sparse inputs. The communication and computation costs scale with the ambient dimension $d$ rather than with the sparsity of the $x_i^{(t)}$, making the protocol prohibitively expensive when $\sum_i \|x_i^{(t)}\|_0 \ll nd$.

(d) Lack of input validation. There is no efficient mechanism to enforce well-formedness of the client contributions $x_i^{(t)}$ (e.g., bounded norm, membership in a prescribed set, or correct encoding) so as to defend against malicious clients submitting arbitrary inputs to corrupt the aggregate.

Problem. Construct a secure aggregation protocol for computing $S^{(t)} = \sum_{i=1}^n x_i^{(t)}$ that simultaneously:

1. Provides provable security against a malicious (stronger than semi-honest) server, including during any setup phase;
2. Hides the per-round aggregate from the server beyond what is necessary for the learning task (for example by combining secure aggregation with a formal notion such as differential privacy on the aggregate or by revealing only multi-round/processed quantities);
3. Achieves communication and computation cost that scales with the effective sparsity of the inputs rather than the ambient dimension $d$;
4. Allows the server (or the protocol participants collectively) to efficiently verify well-formedness of each client's input $x_i^{(t)}$ with respect to a prescribed predicate;
5. Remains efficient and robust to client dropouts at federated-learning scale (large $n$, large $d$).

Either exhibit such a protocol, with formal security definitions and proofs and concrete asymptotic complexity bounds, or prove an impossibility/lower bound showing that the four properties cannot all be achieved simultaneously under standard cryptographic assumptions.

Consider a visible-light communication (VLC) and visible-light positioning (VLP) system intended to support social-distancing applications, including: (i) indoor localization and navigation of users, (ii) crowd density monitoring, (iii) assistance systems for visually impaired or vulnerable individuals, and (iv) outdoor traffic-control communication between intelligent traffic infrastructure and vehicles. The system uses LED transmitters that modulate intensity to convey data, and photodetector receivers that estimate distance/position from the received signal strength (RSS) of the optical signal.

A fundamental limitation is that ambient illumination and direct or diffuse sunlight act as additive optical noise and shot-noise sources on the VLC channel. Concretely, let the received photocurrent at a receiver be
   y(t) = R · h · x(t) + n_amb(t) + n_th(t),
where x(t) is the transmitted optical intensity, h is the channel DC gain, R is the photodetector responsivity, n_th(t) is thermal/electronic noise, and n_amb(t) is shot noise induced by ambient/solar irradiance whose variance can dominate n_th in outdoor or sunlit indoor settings. RSS-based ranging estimates h (and hence distance) from the time-averaged signal power, so unmodeled or time-varying ambient bias in n_amb directly corrupts position estimates and degrades link reliability.

Problem. Design and analyze a VLC/VLP architecture (jointly specifying modulation/coding, optical filtering, receiver signal processing, and positioning algorithm) that simultaneously achieves all of the following under strong and time-varying ambient-light and sunlight interference:

1. Reliable indoor localization and navigation with bounded position error (e.g., a guaranteed upper bound on RMS positioning error as a function of ambient irradiance level and SNR);
2. Accurate crowd-density monitoring derived from VLP measurements that remains consistent in the presence of ambient noise;
3. A robust assistance-system communication link maintaining a target bit-error rate under ambient interference;
4. An outdoor VLC link between traffic-control infrastructure and vehicles that maintains a specified data rate and BER under direct-sunlight conditions.

Provide rigorous performance guarantees (BER, achievable rate, and positioning Cramér–Rao-type lower bounds) for the proposed scheme as functions of ambient noise statistics, and demonstrate that the scheme attains these guarantees, or otherwise establish fundamental limits showing such guarantees cannot be met.

Consider a chemical reaction network (CRN) consisting of a finite set of chemical species $\{X_i\}_{i=1}^N$ and a finite set of elementary reversible reactions $\{\rho\}_{\rho=1}^R$ described by a stoichiometric matrix $S \in \mathbb{Z}^{N \times R}$, where $S_{i\rho}$ is the net change in the abundance of species $X_i$ due to one occurrence of reaction $\rho$. The deterministic dynamics of concentrations $z_i(t)$ obey $\dot z = S\, j(z)$, where $j_\rho(z) = j_\rho^+(z) - j_\rho^-(z)$ are mass-action currents satisfying local detailed balance: $\ln\bigl(j_\rho^+(z)/j_\rho^-(z)\bigr) = -\Delta_\rho\mu(z)/(k_BT)$, where $\mu_i(z) = \mu_i^\circ + k_BT \ln z_i$ are chemical potentials and $\Delta_\rho\mu = \sum_i S_{i\rho}\mu_i$. A subset $Y \subseteq \{1,\dots,N\}$ of species is chemostatted, with concentrations held fixed by external reservoirs.

For such a (generally nonlinear) open CRN, develop a systematic framework of nonequilibrium thermodynamics that:

(a) identifies a complete and minimal set of independent thermodynamic forces — linear combinations of the chemical potentials of the chemostatted species, determined by the cokernel structure of $S$ restricted to $Y$ — that drive the steady-state molecular currents and account for the entropy production rate $\dot\Sigma \ge 0$;

(b) constructs an explicit nonequilibrium free-energy (semistate) functional $\mathcal{F}(z)$, depending on the network topology and on the choice of chemostatted species, that quantifies the free energy stored inside the CRN, satisfies a second-law inequality $\dot{\mathcal{F}} \le \dot W_{\text{driv}} - T\dot\Sigma$ for an appropriately defined driving work $\dot W_{\text{driv}}$, and reduces to the standard relative entropy when the system is detailed-balanced;

(c) defines, for arbitrary partitions of the reactions into "input" and "output" subnetworks, a thermodynamically consistent notion of energy transduction efficiency $\eta$ between coupled chemical processes operating far from equilibrium, and characterizes the topological conditions on $(S,Y)$ under which $\eta$ is bounded away from $0$ and approaches its theoretical maximum;

(d) applies this framework to large metabolic networks to relate quantitative performance measures (dissipation, stored free energy, transduction efficiency, robustness of stationary fluxes) to graph-theoretic and stoichiometric invariants of $(S,Y)$, such as the dimensions of $\ker S$, $\mathrm{coker}\, S$, and the cycle/cocycle decomposition of the reaction hypergraph.

Provide such a formulation, or determine the maximal class of nonlinear CRNs for which (a)–(d) admit a fully consistent and computable solution.

Consider a chemical reaction network (CRN) modeled as a finite set of chemical species $\{X_i\}_{i=1}^N$ together with a finite set of elementary reactions $\{\rho\}$, each written as $\sum_i \nu^-_{i\rho} X_i \rightleftharpoons \sum_i \nu^+_{i\rho} X_i$ with stoichiometric matrix $S_{i\rho} = \nu^+_{i\rho} - \nu^-_{i\rho}$. In the standard setting, the network evolves in a homogeneous, isothermal, dilute (ideal) solution at fixed temperature $T$, and a consistent nonequilibrium thermodynamic framework assigns to each reaction a chemical affinity, an entropy production rate $\dot\Sigma \ge 0$, and a free-energy balance derived from local detailed balance with rate constants satisfying $k^+_\rho/k^-_\rho = \exp\!\big(-\sum_i S_{i\rho}\mu^\circ_i/(k_B T)\big)$, where $\mu^\circ_i$ are standard chemical potentials.

Develop a mathematically consistent extension of this nonequilibrium thermodynamic framework for CRNs that simultaneously incorporates:
(a) nonisothermal effects, in which the temperature $T(\mathbf r,t)$ is a dynamical field coupled to chemical kinetics through reaction enthalpies and a heat equation;
(b) non-ideal solutions, in which the chemical potentials take the form $\mu_i = \mu^\circ_i + k_B T \ln a_i(\{c_j\},T)$ with activities $a_i$ derived from a non-ideal free-energy density (e.g.\ Flory–Huggins or Debye–Hückel), and reaction rates and local detailed balance are modified accordingly;
(c) photochemistry, in which a subset of reactions is driven by absorption/emission of photons from a radiation field with prescribed spectral intensity, with the corresponding entropy and free-energy exchange properly accounted for;
(d) electrochemistry, in which charged species couple to an electrostatic potential $\phi(\mathbf r,t)$ determined self-consistently (e.g.\ via Poisson–Nernst–Planck equations), and reaction affinities include the electrochemical potential $\tilde\mu_i = \mu_i + z_i e\phi$.

The required output is: a unified set of dynamical equations for concentrations, temperature, and electrostatic/radiation fields; a definition of total entropy production rate that is non-negative and decomposes additively into chemical, thermal, photochemical, and electrochemical contributions; a corresponding free-energy/Lyapunov functional whose dissipation equals the entropy production at the appropriate temperature; and proofs that, in suitable closed/detailed-balanced limits, the framework reduces to the standard isothermal ideal-solution CRN thermodynamics. Establish, in particular, the analogue of the second law and of the fluctuation theorems in this combined setting.

Consider the problem of modeling the interiors of ice-giant planets having masses and radii comparable to Uranus (mass ≈ 14.5 M_⊕, radius ≈ 25 559 km) and Neptune (mass ≈ 17.1 M_⊕, radius ≈ 24 622 km), under conditions of pressures up to several hundred GPa and temperatures up to several thousand K characteristic of their deep interiors. Let the candidate constituents be: water (H₂O), ammonia (NH₃), methane (CH₄), rock (a silicate/iron component), hydrogen (H₂), and helium (He). For each pure component i denote by P_i(ρ,T) its equation of state (EOS) relating pressure, density and temperature, and for any mixture with mass fractions {x_i} denote by P_mix(ρ,T,{x_i}) the corresponding mixture EOS, including possible non-ideal corrections, phase boundaries (fluid, superionic, plasma, demixing), and chemical reactions among components.

Determine, with quantitative accuracy sufficient to constrain interior structure models that match the observed mass, radius, gravitational moments J₂, J₄, and rotation rate of Uranus and Neptune:

(a) the pure-component high-pressure–temperature EOSs P_i(ρ,T) for H₂O, NH₃, CH₄, rock, H₂, and He throughout the relevant (P,T) domain;
(b) the mixing rules / non-ideal mixture EOSs P_mix for the binary and multi-component mixtures (H₂O+NH₃+CH₄), (ices+rock), and (ices+H₂+He), including miscibility gaps and conditions for demixing or differentiation;
(c) the chemical interactions and phase transitions among these species (e.g. dissociation, ionization, superionic phases, formation of new compounds) that occur in the relevant (P,T) regime;

so that interior structure models built from this input can rigorously exclude compositional profiles inconsistent with the observed bulk and gravitational data, and can determine which materials are mixed versus differentiated within the planets.

Consider the Weihrauch lattice, whose elements are equivalence classes of (multi-valued) partial functions f:⊆X⇉Y between represented spaces under Weihrauch reducibility ≤_W (where f ≤_W g iff there exist computable functionals H,K such that whenever G realizes g, the map p ↦ H(p, G(K(p))) realizes f). On this structure one considers the following operations:

- the binary supremum ⊔ and infimum ⊓ with respect to ≤_W;
- the parallel product × (taking (f, g) to f × g acting componentwise);
- the finite-parallelization ∗ (with f^∗ = ⊔_{n∈ℕ} f^{(n)});
- the compositional product ⋆ (with f ⋆ g being the maximum, under ≤_W, of all composites f' ∘ g' with f' ≤_W f, g' ≤_W g);
- the diamond operator ⋄ (the iteration / total-Kleene-star operation);
- the implication → defined via f → g = max_{≤_W} { h : g ≤_W f ⋆ h };
- the constant 1 (the identity on a one-point space);
- the operator b (the "first-order part" / boundedness operator, assigning to each problem f its largest first-order Weihrauch degree below f).

It is known that ⊔ and ⊓ are definable from ≤_W; that ∗ is definable from 1, ≤_W and ×; that ⋄ is definable from 1, ≤_W and ⋆; and that → is definable from ⋆ and ≤_W.

Question: Is the operator b definable from the remaining structure (i.e., from ≤_W together with the operations 1, ×, ⋆, ⋄, →, ⊔, ⊓)?

Let $(K_i)_{i\ge 1}$ be a sequence of compact subsets of $\mathbb{R}$ satisfying:
(i) $0 \in K_i$ for every $i \ge 1$;
(ii) every element of every $K_i$ is nonnegative;
(iii) $\operatorname{diam}(K_i) > 0$ for every $i \ge 1$;
(iv) $\operatorname{diam}(K_i) \to 0$ as $i \to \infty$.

Define the achievement set
$$ S \;=\; \Big\{\, \sum_{j=1}^{\infty} x_j \;:\; x_j \in K_j \text{ for every } j \,\Big\} \subseteq \mathbb{R}, $$
and, for each integer $z \ge 0$, the tail set
$$ R_z \;=\; \Big\{\, \sum_{i=z+1}^{\infty} x_i \;:\; x_i \in K_i \text{ for every } i > z \,\Big\}. $$

Characterize, up to homeomorphism, all topological spaces that can arise as $S$ for some such sequence $(K_i)_{i\ge 1}$.

Let X be a smooth complex projective variety. A vector bundle E on X is called asymptotically generically generated if, for all sufficiently large m, the evaluation map H^0(X, Sym^m E) ⊗ O_X → Sym^m E is surjective on a non-empty Zariski-open subset of X. For such an E, define the Kodaira-type rational map φ_m : X ⇢ Grass(rk Sym^m E - h^0(Sym^m E), Sym^m E*) (or, equivalently, the rational map to projective space induced by sections of Sym^m E on the projectivization P(E)), and define the Iitaka dimension κ(E) of E as the maximum, over m ≫ 0, of the dimension of the image of the associated stabilized Kodaira map; equivalently, κ(E) := κ(O_{P(E)}(1)) when this tautological line bundle is asymptotically generically generated.

Fix numerical invariants — for instance, a rank r ≥ 1 and Chern classes c_1, c_2, … — such that there exists a (coarse) moduli space M = M_X(r; c_1, c_2, …) of Gieseker-semistable vector bundles on X with these invariants. Let M^s ⊂ M denote the open subset parametrizing stable bundles, and assume that every E ∈ M^s for which κ(E) is defined is asymptotically generically generated.

For each integer k ∈ {0, 1, …, dim X + r - 1}, set
   M^s_k := { [E] ∈ M^s : E is asymptotically generically generated and κ(E) = k }.

Investigate the geometric properties of the resulting stratification {M^s_k}_k of M^s. In particular, describe:
(a) which strata are non-empty, and the dimension of each non-empty stratum;
(b) whether each M^s_k is locally closed, constructible, or has a natural scheme structure, and what the closure relations among the strata are;
(c) the behaviour of κ in families, e.g. its semicontinuity properties on M^s;
(d) the geometry (irreducibility, smoothness, singularities) of the strata and of their closures.

Let $X$ be a smooth complex projective variety and fix numerical invariants (a Hilbert polynomial with respect to a fixed ample polarization, or a Chern character). Let $M = M_X(v)$ denote the Gieseker moduli space of $S$-equivalence classes of Gieseker semistable coherent sheaves on $X$ with these invariants. Recall that a semistable sheaf $E$ is called properly semistable if it is not stable; equivalently, $E$ admits a Jordan–Hölder filtration whose graded object $\mathrm{gr}(E) = \bigoplus_i E_i$ is a non-trivial direct sum of stable sheaves of the same reduced Hilbert polynomial as $E$, and the $S$-equivalence class of $E$ is represented by $\mathrm{gr}(E)$.

Assume that a notion of Iitaka dimension $\kappa(E) \in \{-\infty, 0, 1, \dots, \dim X\}$ has been defined for properly semistable sheaves on $X$ in such a way that it depends only on the $S$-equivalence class (so that it descends to a function $\kappa : M^{\mathrm{pss}} \to \{-\infty, 0, 1, \dots, \dim X\}$, where $M^{\mathrm{pss}} \subset M$ is the closed subset parametrizing $S$-equivalence classes of properly semistable sheaves).

For each $d \in \{-\infty, 0, 1, \dots, \dim X\}$, define the Iitaka stratum
$$M^{\mathrm{pss}}_d := \{[E] \in M^{\mathrm{pss}} : \kappa(E) = d\}.$$

Determine the geometric features of the resulting stratification $M^{\mathrm{pss}} = \bigsqcup_d M^{\mathrm{pss}}_d$. In particular, describe:
(i) whether each stratum $M^{\mathrm{pss}}_d$ is locally closed (and the closure relations among strata);
(ii) the dimensions of the strata, and whether they admit natural scheme or stack structures;
(iii) singularities and irreducible component structure of the strata;
(iv) how the stratification interacts with the Jordan–Hölder decomposition, i.e. with the natural maps from products of moduli spaces of stable factors to $M^{\mathrm{pss}}$.

Let $X$ be a complex algebraic variety (or complex manifold) and let $Y \subset X$ be a contractible subvariety, i.e., a subvariety admitting a proper birational contraction $\pi: X \to X'$ with $\pi(Y)$ a point and $\pi$ an isomorphism on $X \setminus Y$. Given a vector bundle (or, more generally, a coherent sheaf) $\mathcal{E}$ defined on a neighborhood of $Y$ in $X$, one wishes to attach to the pair $(\mathcal{E}, Y)$ a collection of "local characteristic classes" or local numerical invariants (generalizing, for example, the local holomorphic Euler characteristic $\chi_{\mathrm{loc}}(\mathcal{E}, Y) = h^0(Y, R^0\pi_*\mathcal{E}) + \cdots$ studied for contractible curves and rational surfaces in threefolds).

Formulate a complete set of axioms that such a general theory of local characteristic classes should satisfy. The axioms should specify:

(a) the target group/ring in which the local classes take values, and how it depends on $(X, Y)$;
(b) functorial behavior under pullback by morphisms $f: (X_1, Y_1) \to (X_2, Y_2)$ that respect the contractible loci;
(c) Whitney-type additivity in short exact sequences $0 \to \mathcal{E}' \to \mathcal{E} \to \mathcal{E}'' \to 0$ of sheaves near $Y$;
(d) a normalization recovering the classical (global) Chern, Euler, or related characteristic classes when $Y = X$ is compact, and recovering the local holomorphic Euler characteristic in the known surface/threefold cases;
(e) compatibility with the contraction $\pi: X \to X'$ (a "pushforward / blow-down" axiom relating the local classes on $X$ along $Y$ to invariants of $\pi_*\mathcal{E}$ at $\pi(Y)$);
(f) a uniqueness statement: the axioms should determine the local classes uniquely.

Show that such a set of axioms exists and that it characterizes a unique theory of local characteristic classes of vector bundles and coherent sheaves around contractible subvarieties.

Let F_2 denote the binary field. Fix a positive integer n and a polynomial f(x) = x^n − a_{n−1}x^{n−1} − ⋯ − a_1 x − a_0 in F_2[x] with a_0 ≠ 0. The polycyclic code associated with f is, by definition, an ideal of the quotient ring F_2[x]/⟨f(x)⟩, identified with a linear subspace C ⊆ F_2^n via the standard coefficient correspondence. Equip F_2^n with the usual Euclidean inner product ⟨u,v⟩ = Σ u_i v_i, and let C^⊥ = { v ∈ F_2^n : ⟨u,v⟩ = 0 for all u ∈ C } denote the dual code. The code C is called:
 • self-dual if C = C^⊥;
 • self-orthogonal if C ⊆ C^⊥;
 • dual-containing if C^⊥ ⊆ C.

Question: Does there exist any choice of n ≥ 1 and of f(x) ∈ F_2[x] (monic, with nonzero constant term) such that the associated polycyclic code C ⊆ F_2^n is self-dual, self-orthogonal, or dual-containing? Equivalently, prove or disprove the statement that no nontrivial polycyclic code over F_2 can be self-dual, self-orthogonal, or dual-containing.

Let (M, g) be a smooth, time-oriented Lorentzian manifold of dimension n ≥ 2 (a spacetime), and let τ_M denote its underlying smooth manifold topology. Several alternative topologies on M have been introduced and studied in the literature, including: the Path topology of Hawking–King–McCarthy, the Alexandrov (interval) topology generated by chronological diamonds I^+(p) ∩ I^-(q), Zeeman's fine topology on Minkowski space and its generalizations, the A-topology, and the space topology. Each is finer than τ_M in suitable cases and is designed to reflect the causal/Lorentzian structure rather than only the differential one.

A "foliation" of M means a codimension-one foliation by spacelike hypersurfaces (Σ_t)_{t ∈ ℝ} arising from a time function t: M → ℝ.

Problem. Construct a topology τ on M such that:

(a) τ is strictly different from τ_M and from each of the alternative topologies listed above (Path, Alexandrov, Zeeman, A-topology, space topology);

(b) τ is "mathematically fruitful" in the sense that (M, τ) admits a well-developed theory of convergence, continuous causal curves, and continuous Lorentzian isometries, and the homeomorphism group of (M, τ) coincides with (or refines in a controlled way) the group of smooth conformal isometries of (M, g);

(c) τ is "physically meaningful" in the sense that the continuous timelike curves in (M, τ) are exactly the (future-directed) timelike curves of (M, g), and the topology distinguishes events that are causally inequivalent;

(d) τ is compatible with a chosen foliation (Σ_t) in the sense that each leaf Σ_t is a closed embedded topological submanifold of (M, τ) and the time function t is continuous with respect to τ.

Formulate explicit criteria characterizing such τ, and either exhibit such a topology or prove that no topology satisfying (a)–(d) exists.

Consider the standard framework of human complex-trait genetics, in which a quantitative or binary phenotype Y measured across a population is modeled as a function of genetic variation G and non-genetic factors E, and in which "narrow-sense heritability" h² denotes the fraction of phenotypic variance attributable to additive genetic effects. Most large-scale association and heritability studies represent each individual's genome as a vector of deviations (single-nucleotide polymorphisms and short indels) from a single linear reference genome, restricted largely to autosomes and to variants that are easily called by short-read sequencing.

Several classes of genetic variation are systematically underrepresented or absent from this representation, including:
(i) variation on the sex chromosomes X and Y;
(ii) mitochondrial DNA variation;
(iii) large structural variants and copy-number variants;
(iv) variation in repetitive regions (e.g., tandem repeats, segmental duplications, centromeric and telomeric regions);
(v) haplotype phase information;
(vi) somatic mosaicism (genetic differences between cells within an individual);
(vii) sequence and structural content not representable as deviations from a single reference, such as that captured by pangenome graphs or personal de novo assemblies.

Quantify, for a representative set of human complex traits, the contribution of each of the above classes of variation to total narrow-sense heritability, and determine the extent to which they collectively account for the gap ("missing heritability") between pedigree-based estimates of h² and the heritability captured by current genome-wide association studies. Furthermore, propose and justify statistical models and measurement protocols (sequencing, variant calling, encoding, and association testing) under which each of these forms of variation can be incorporated into genetic studies, and decide in particular whether somatic mosaicism is best modeled as a heritable genetic component or treated as a non-genetic (environmental/omic) factor.

For a quantitative or binary human trait Y measured on a population, three estimands are commonly considered:

(i) Twin-based (broad-sense) heritability H², estimated from concordance/correlation differences between monozygotic and dizygotic twin pairs;

(ii) SNP-based (genotype) heritability h²_SNP, defined as the proportion of phenotypic variance attributable to additive effects of common single-nucleotide polymorphisms genotyped on standard arrays, typically estimated by methods such as variance-component (GREML) or LD-score regression;

(iii) The variance R² explained by a concrete polygenic risk score (PRS), i.e. a linear predictor Σ_j β̂_j X_j whose weights β̂_j are estimated from genome-wide association summary statistics on a training cohort and evaluated out-of-sample.

Empirically, for most complex traits one observes the strict inequalities

   R²(PRS) < h²_SNP < H².

The problem is to give a quantitative, mechanistic account of the two gaps H² − h²_SNP (the "still-missing" heritability) and h²_SNP − R²(PRS) (the "still-hidden" heritability). Specifically: determine to what extent each of the following candidate sources contributes to these gaps, and whether they together suffice to close them:

(a) additive contributions of rare and low-frequency variants and structural variants not tagged by common SNP arrays;
(b) non-additive genetic effects (dominance, epistasis, gene–gene interactions);
(c) gene–environment interactions and gene–environment correlation;
(d) residual population structure and assortative mating biasing variance-component estimators;
(e) environmental confounding and shared-environment biases inflating twin-based estimates;
(f) indirect genetic effects from relatives (genetic nurture);
(g) limitations of the prediction model class (e.g. sparsity, linearity, training sample size) used to construct the PRS;
(h) the possibility that "heritability," as classically defined under the additive polygenic model, is itself misinterpreted or ill-posed for the traits in question.

Provide either a decomposition theorem/empirical accounting that closes the inequalities, or rigorous lower bounds showing which of (a)–(h) are individually necessary or sufficient.

Consider the setting of statistical and functional genetics in humans, where a quantitative or disease trait T is influenced by many genetic variants distributed across the genome. Suppose that for a given trait T, a particular variant v (a single-nucleotide polymorphism, indel, or structural variant) or a gene g has been causally linked to T, in the sense that interventions on v or g would change the distribution of T at the population level. Assume further that:

(i) most such causal variants v reside in noncoding regions of the genome (i.e., outside protein-coding exons), and individually exert only weak effects on T;
(ii) the trait T is highly polygenic, or even omnigenic, in the sense that a very large number of variants—potentially across most expressed genes—contribute small, often comparable effects to T;
(iii) controlled, randomized, organism-level perturbation experiments in living humans are not feasible, so any mechanistic inference must rely on observational human data, model systems (cell lines, organoids, model organisms), and indirect or in vitro perturbation assays.

Devise general analytical and/or experimental strategies that, given such a causal variant v or causal gene g for trait T, identify or substantially narrow down the biological mechanism—i.e., the chain of molecular, cellular, tissue-level, and physiological processes—through which v or g influences T. A successful strategy should:

(a) be applicable to weak-effect noncoding variants whose direct molecular consequence is not immediately apparent;
(b) account for the polygenic/omnigenic architecture of T, including the possibility that the relevant mechanism operates through diffuse regulatory networks rather than a single core pathway;
(c) make precise, testable predictions about intermediate molecular phenotypes (e.g., chromatin state, transcript abundance, protein activity, cell-state composition) that can be validated using human cell or tissue assays, model organisms, or population-scale multi-omic data;
(d) come with a principled framework for evaluating when a proposed mechanism is established, including criteria distinguishing genuine mechanistic links from correlative associations.

Provide such a strategy, or prove rigorous obstructions showing that, under stated assumptions about the available data and assays, no strategy in a specified class can recover the mechanism.

Let $C$ be a smooth irreducible projective curve of genus $g \ge 2$ over $\mathbb{C}$. A coherent system of type $(n,d,k)$ on $C$ is a pair $(E,V)$ where $E$ is a vector bundle on $C$ of rank $n$ and degree $d$, and $V \subseteq H^0(E)$ is a linear subspace of dimension $k$. For a real parameter $\alpha$, the $\alpha$-slope of $(E,V)$ is $\mu_\alpha(E,V) = d/n + \alpha k/n$, and $(E,V)$ is $\alpha$-(semi)stable if for every proper coherent subsystem $(E',V')$ with $0 < \mathrm{rk}(E') < n$ one has $\mu_\alpha(E',V') < \mu_\alpha(E,V)$ (resp.\ $\le$). Let $G(\alpha;n,d,k)$ denote the moduli space of $\alpha$-stable coherent systems of type $(n,d,k)$. Let $B(n,d,k) \subseteq M(n,d)$ denote the Brill–Noether locus of stable bundles $E$ of rank $n$ and degree $d$ with $h^0(E) \ge k$, and let $U(n,d,k)$ denote the locus of $E \in B(n,d,k)$ with $h^0(E) = k$ (i.e., the coherent systems $(E,V)$ with $V = H^0(E)$).

Fix integers $n \ge 2$ and $k \ge 1$. Does there exist an integer $d_0 = d_0(n,k)$ such that all of the following hold simultaneously?
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $G(\alpha;n,d,k) \ne \emptyset$ if and only if $\alpha > 0$, $(n-k)\alpha < d$, and $d \ge d_0$;
\item[(b)] $B(n,d,k) \ne \emptyset$ if and only if $d \ge d_0$;
\item[(c)] if $d \ge d_0$, then $U(n,d,k) \ne \emptyset$.
\end{enumerate}

Consider lattice QCD at finite baryon chemical potential $\mu$, where the Euclidean path integral

$$Z(\mu) = \int \mathcal{D}U \, \det M(U,\mu)\, e^{-S_g(U)}$$

has a complex fermion determinant $\det M(U,\mu) \in \mathbb{C}$ for $\mu \neq 0$, so that the integrand cannot be interpreted as a probability weight (the "sign problem"). A variety of numerical approaches—reweighting from $\mu=0$, Taylor expansion in $\mu/T$, analytic continuation from imaginary chemical potential, complex Langevin dynamics, Lefschetz-thimble integration, density-of-states methods, and others—are formally correct: under their stated mathematical assumptions they would converge to the exact expectation values $\langle \mathcal{O}\rangle_\mu = Z(\mu)^{-1}\int \mathcal{D}U\, \mathcal{O}\, \det M\, e^{-S_g}$. However, in finite-statistics simulations these methods can silently produce systematically biased or unphysical results due to phenomena such as:
(i) overlap problems, where the simulated ensemble has exponentially small overlap with the target ensemble at $\mu \neq 0$;
(ii) uncontrolled fluctuations of the complex phase $e^{i\theta(U,\mu)}$ of the determinant, producing severe sign cancellations;
(iii) for stochastic complexification schemes (e.g.\ complex Langevin), insufficient decay of the probability distribution in the non-compact directions of the complexified configuration space, invalidating the formal proof of correctness;
(iv) for thimble decompositions, missed contributing thimbles or unresolved Stokes phenomena.

The problem is the following. Devise a set of robust, practically computable diagnostic criteria $\{D_i(\mathcal{E})\}$, evaluable from the simulated ensemble $\mathcal{E}$ alone (without knowledge of the exact answer), such that:

(a) if all $D_i$ lie within explicitly specified tolerances, then the computed expectation values $\langle \mathcal{O}\rangle_\mu$ are guaranteed (with quantifiable confidence) to be free of the systematic artifacts (i)–(iv) and to lie within stated error bars of the true value;
(b) if any $D_i$ falls outside its tolerance, the diagnostic identifies which failure mode is responsible;
(c) the criteria apply uniformly across the formally-correct methods listed above and are sharp enough to distinguish trustworthy from untrustworthy results in regimes (such as $\mu/T \gtrsim 1$ in the cold/dense regime of QCD) where current heuristics are known to be inadequate.

Construct such diagnostics, or prove that no diagnostic with properties (a)–(c) exists within a specified computational class.

Fix an integer $
u \ge 3$ and define the map $T:\mathbb{N}	o\mathbb{N}$ by
$$T(n)= egin{cases} n/2 & 	ext{if } n 	ext{ is even},\ ((2^{
u}-1)n+1)/2 & 	ext{if } n 	ext{ is odd}.\end{cases}$$
For $n\in\mathbb{N}$, let the trajectory of $n$ be the sequence $n, T(n), T^2(n), \dots$. Call a finite sequence $(n_0	o n_1	o\cdots	o n_{k-1}	o n_0)$ a cycle of $T$ if $T(n_i)=n_{i+1 mod k}$ for all $i$, and let $\mathcal{C}$ denote the set of all cycles of $T$ in $\mathbb{N}$. Note that
$$\Omega(1):\quad 1	o 2^{
u}-1	o 2^{
u-2}\cdot	ext{(odd)}	o\cdots	o 1,$$
more precisely the cycle $1	o 2^{
u}-1	o 2^{
u-2}	o 2^{
u-3}	o \cdots 	o 2	o 1$ of length $
u$, is always a cycle of $T$. Let $G(1)=\{n\in\mathbb{N}:	ext{the trajectory of } n 	ext{ eventually enters } \Omega(1)\}$ and $G(\infty)=\{n\in\mathbb{N}:T^k(n)	o\infty 	ext{ as } k	o\infty\}$.

Prove or disprove the following two statements for every integer $
u\ge 3$:
(a) $\mathcal{C}=\{\Omega(1)\}$, i.e. $\Omega(1)$ is the only cycle of $T$ in $\mathbb{N}$.
(b) $\mathbb{N}=G(1)\cup G(\infty)$ with $G(\infty)
e\emptyset$ (so every positive integer either reaches the trivial cycle or has a divergent trajectory, and divergent trajectories exist).

status_search_result:
This is a conjecture in the family of generalized Collatz / Syracuse problems with multiplier $a=2^{
u}-1$ and additive constant $b=1$. Even the classical case $
u=2$ (the $3n+1$ problem) is famously open, and no resolution is known for the $(2^{
u}-1)n+1$ generalizations for $
u\ge 3$. No published solution was located.

self_contained_problem:
Consider a Markov game with a finite set of agents $\mathcal{N} = \{1, \dots, n\}$, state space $\mathcal{S}$, joint action space $\mathcal{A} = \prod_{i=1}^n \mathcal{A}_i$, transition kernel $P(s' \mid s, a)$, and per-agent reward functions $r_i(s, a)$. Suppose we are given an observational dataset $\mathcal{D} = \{(s_t, a_t, r_t, s_{t+1})\}$ generated by a population of trajectories in which:

(i) the data are collected under an unknown mixture of behavior policies $\mu = \sum_k w_k \mu_k$ over the joint action space, with neither the mixture weights $w_k$ nor the component policies $\mu_k$ observed;
(ii) only a subset $\mathcal{O} \subseteq \mathcal{N}$ of agents' actions are recorded, while the actions of the remaining agents in $\mathcal{N} \setminus \mathcal{O}$ are unobserved confounders that may influence both the recorded actions and the environment dynamics;
(iii) a causal directed acyclic graph $\mathcal{G}$ encoding qualitative assumptions about the dependencies among states, observed actions, hidden agents' actions, and rewards is provided, possibly together with sensitivity parameters bounding the strength of unmeasured confounding.

Design a learning procedure that, given $\mathcal{D}$ and $\mathcal{G}$, returns for each evaluation joint policy $\pi$ a confidence interval $[\underline{V}^\pi, \overline{V}^\pi]$ on its expected return $V^\pi = \mathbb{E}_\pi igl[\sum_{t \ge 0} \gamma^t \sum_i r_i(s_t, a_t) igr]$ such that:

(a) the interval is valid (covers $V^\pi$ with prescribed probability) under the stated causal assumptions and the sensitivity bounds on hidden confounding;
(b) the interval is sharp, in the sense that it equals the tightest possible bound implied by the assumptions and the observational distribution;
(c) using these intervals, an offline policy-optimization rule outputs a joint policy $\hat{\pi}$ whose suboptimality with respect to $\max_\pi V^\pi$ admits a finite-sample bound that scales gracefully with the number of agents $n$ and degrades smoothly as the confounding sensitivity parameters grow.

Provide such a procedure together with the corresponding identification, estimation, and regret guarantees, or prove that no such procedure exists under the given assumptions.

In multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL), a system consists of N agents interacting in a Markov (or stochastic) game (S, {A_i}, P, {R_i}, γ), where S is a state space, A_i is the action space of agent i, P: S × A_1 × ... × A_N → Δ(S) is a transition kernel, R_i is the reward of agent i, and γ ∈ [0,1) is a discount factor. Causal MARL augments this setting by positing causal assumptions (e.g., a structural causal model (SCM) M = (U, V, F, P(U)) with exogenous variables U, endogenous variables V, structural functions F, and an associated directed acyclic graph G) over the variables describing states, actions, rewards, and possibly latent confounders, in order to support reasoning about interventions do(·) and counterfactuals.

Most existing causal MARL formulations rely on graphical SCMs. However, the broader causal inference literature offers alternative frameworks, including:
(a) the Neyman–Rubin potential outcomes framework, in which each unit i and treatment a has a potential outcome Y_i(a), with causal effects defined as contrasts E[Y(a) − Y(a')];
(b) propensity score methods, where the propensity score e(x) = Pr(A = 1 | X = x) is used to adjust for confounding in observational data;
(c) instrumental variable (IV) methods, in which a variable Z affects the treatment A but influences the outcome Y only through A, enabling identification of causal effects under unobserved confounding;
(d) frameworks linking differential equations (ODEs/SDEs) to causal models, where the equilibria or transient dynamics of dx/dt = f(x, u) are interpreted as inducing structural causal relations among the components of x.

Develop a principled framework for causal MARL that incorporates one or more of the non-graphical causal modeling paradigms (a)–(d) above. Specifically:

1. Define a formal model that integrates such a paradigm with a multi-agent Markov game, specifying which quantities play the role of treatments, outcomes, instruments, propensity scores, or dynamical states, and how multiple agents' simultaneous interventions are handled.

2. State sufficient identifiability conditions under which causal effects of joint or individual agent policies on per-agent or social rewards are identifiable from data generated by the multi-agent system (possibly off-policy and with unobserved confounding among agents).

3. Provide algorithms (with finite-sample or asymptotic guarantees) that exploit these assumptions for policy evaluation and/or policy optimization in MARL, and demonstrate that the explicit causal assumptions yield provable benefits (e.g., reduced sample complexity, improved off-policy evaluation, robustness to distribution shift, or improved coordination) compared to non-causal MARL baselines.

A Frobenius manifold is a smooth (or complex) manifold $M$ equipped with the following data:
(i) a commutative, associative, unital product $\circ$ on each tangent space $T_pM$, depending smoothly on $p$;
(ii) a flat (pseudo-)Riemannian metric $g$ on $M$ such that the structure constants of $\circ$ in flat coordinates are derivatives of a single function (the potential $F$), i.e. there exist flat coordinates $(t^1,\dots,t^n)$ in which $c_{ijk}=\partial_i\partial_j\partial_k F$ and $\eta_{ij}\,c^i{}_{jk}\eta^{kl}=\partial_j\partial_k\partial_l F\cdot\eta\text{-raisings}$;
(iii) a unit vector field $e$ which is $g$-flat and satisfies $e\circ X=X$ for all $X$;
(iv) an Euler vector field $E$ giving $F$ the appropriate quasi-homogeneity, with $\mathcal{L}_E\,g=(2-d)g$ for some constant $d$.

Two torsion-free affine connections $\nabla$ and $\widetilde\nabla$ on $M$ are projectively equivalent if they share the same unparametrised geodesics; equivalently, $\widetilde\nabla_X Y=\nabla_X Y+\alpha(X)Y+\alpha(Y)X$ for some $1$-form $\alpha$. A geometric structure on $M$ is called projectively invariant if it depends only on the projective class of the Levi-Civita connection of $g$ rather than on $g$ itself.

Questions:
(a) Is the Frobenius structure $(M,\circ,e,E,g)$ projectively invariant, in the sense that the multiplication $\circ$, unit $e$, Euler field $E$, and the associated WDVV/flat-pencil data depend only on the projective class of the Levi-Civita connection of $g$?
(b) Can the flatness assumption on $g$ in the definition of a Frobenius manifold be relaxed to require only that $g$ have constant sectional curvature, while preserving a coherent theory analogous to the standard Frobenius manifold theory (in particular admitting a potential $F$, flat pencil of metrics, and Dubrovin connection)?

Let $(W,S)$ be a Coxeter system, with Bruhat order $\le$ on $W$ and identity element $e$. For each pair $u,v \in W$ with $u \le v$, let $P_{u,v}(q) \in \mathbb{Z}[q]$ denote the associated Kazhdan–Lusztig polynomial, defined as the unique family of polynomials satisfying:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $P_{u,u}(q) = 1$ for all $u \in W$, and $P_{u,v}(q) = 0$ unless $u \le v$;
\item $\deg P_{u,v}(q) \le \tfrac{1}{2}(\ell(v) - \ell(u) - 1)$ when $u < v$, where $\ell$ is the Coxeter length;
\item the recursion arising from the Hecke algebra of $(W,S)$, equivalently
$$q^{\ell(v)-\ell(u)} P_{u,v}(q^{-1}) = \sum_{u \le z \le v} R_{u,z}(q)\, P_{z,v}(q),$$
where $R_{u,z}(q)$ are the Kazhdan–Lusztig $R$-polynomials.
\end{enumerate}

Question: For every Coxeter system $(W,S)$ and every pair $u,v \in W$ with $u \le v$, do there exist a Coxeter system $(W',S')$ and an element $w \in W'$ such that
$$P_{u,v}(q) \;=\; P_{e,w}(q)\quad \text{in } \mathbb{Z}[q],$$
where $e$ denotes the identity of $W'$?

Consider a three-dimensional two-component Fermi gas consisting of "spin-up" fermions of mass m↑ and "spin-down" fermions of mass m↓, interacting only via ↑↓ s-wave interactions tuned near a magnetic Feshbach resonance. Assume the mass ratio m↑/m↓ is sufficiently large that the Efimov effect occurs in the three-body (and more generally N-body) sector with two ↑ fermions and one ↓ fermion (and their natural N-body extensions): there exist Efimovian N-body bound states (N ≥ 3) whose spectrum, in the strict zero-range limit, is unbounded from below (Thomas collapse) and whose three-body recombination rate diverges. Now model the interaction by a narrow Feshbach resonance, characterized by a finite, negative effective range r_e (equivalently, a microscopic length b → 0 limit in which the effective range remains nonzero), so that:
(i) the N-body spectrum becomes bounded from below;
(ii) the three-body loss rate tends to zero in the relevant limit;
(iii) the Efimovian discrete-scaling bound-state structure persists at moderate energies.

Question: Determine whether, under these conditions, long-lived Efimovian N-body bound states can be prepared and experimentally observed (i.e., have lifetimes much longer than typical experimental and trap timescales) in such a cold-atom Fermi gas with a sufficiently large mass ratio and a narrow Feshbach resonance providing a stabilizing nonzero effective range. Equivalently, exhibit a concrete realization (atomic species, mass ratio, resonance parameters) and demonstrate—theoretically and experimentally—the stable preparation and observation of these Efimovian N-body states.

Let $G$ be a 3-connected cubic (3-regular) graph. A blue-red vertex coloring of $G$ is a partition $V(G) = V_B \cup V_R$ of its vertex set into two classes (blue and red); let $B$ and $R$ denote the subgraphs of $G$ induced by $V_B$ and $V_R$, respectively. Such a coloring is called crumby if it satisfies all three of the following conditions:
(i) $\Delta(B) \le 1$, i.e., the blue induced subgraph is a matching together with isolated vertices;
(ii) $\delta(R) \ge 1$, i.e., every red vertex has at least one red neighbor;
(iii) $R$ contains no path with 3 edges (i.e., no $P_4$ as a subgraph).

It is known that there exist 3-connected cubic graphs admitting no crumby coloring, so condition (iii) (combined with (i) and (ii)) cannot be guaranteed for every 3-connected cubic graph. 

Problem: Find a relaxation of the conditions imposed on the red induced subgraph $R$ — that is, a weaker property $\mathcal{P}(R)$ replacing the requirement "$\delta(R) \ge 1$ and $R$ contains no 3-edge path" — such that every 3-connected cubic graph $G$ admits a blue-red vertex coloring with $\Delta(B) \le 1$ and with $R$ satisfying $\mathcal{P}(R)$.

Consider cosmological models that extend the Standard Model of particle physics (BSM cosmology), in which new physical phenomena (e.g., new particles, fields, primordial black holes, or modified early-universe dynamics) leave imprints on a variety of observational channels. Suppose that one or more of the following experimental hints are independently confirmed as genuine signals of beyond-the-Standard-Model physics:

 (a) an annual-modulation signal of the type reported in direct dark-matter detection with NaI(Tl) targets (DAMA-type experiments);
 (b) gravitational-wave events observed by ground-based interferometers (LIGO–Virgo–KAGRA) whose source population or waveforms cannot be accounted for by Standard Model astrophysics;
 (c) a stochastic gravitational-wave background detected by pulsar timing arrays in the nanohertz band;
 (d) populations of unexpectedly massive or unexpectedly early galaxies at high redshift observed by infrared space telescopes (JWST-type observations);
 (e) a confirmed antihelium component in cosmic rays of the type searched for by space-based magnetic spectrometers (AMS-02-type experiments).

 Develop a systematic methodology for reanalysing existing observational constraints on cosmological parameters and on BSM model parameters under the assumption that any subset of (a)–(e) is confirmed. Specifically:

 1. Formulate quantitatively how each confirmed signal modifies the prior probability distributions and likelihood functions used to derive cosmological bounds (e.g., on dark matter properties, primordial power spectrum, expansion history, baryogenesis scenarios, primordial black hole abundance).
 2. Determine how the “edges” (i.e., boundary regions) of current exclusion contours and confidence regions should be reinterpreted when a previously ignored BSM contribution is included as a genuine effect rather than as a systematic to be marginalized away.
 3. Identify which combinations of (a)–(e), if simultaneously confirmed, lead to mutually consistent BSM cosmological scenarios, and characterize the resulting allowed regions of parameter space.

 Provide a coherent reanalysis framework, or otherwise establish obstructions to such a framework.

Titan, Saturn's largest moon, possesses a dense nitrogen-dominated atmosphere with roughly 1.5 bar surface pressure and a few percent methane (CH4) at the surface. Photochemical and escape modeling indicates that atmospheric CH4 is irreversibly destroyed by ultraviolet-driven photolysis and conversion to higher hydrocarbons (which are then deposited on the surface), with a characteristic depletion timescale of order a few times 10^7 years — far shorter than the ~4.5 Gyr age of the Solar System. Determine, with quantitative justification:

(a) When (in absolute time before present) and by what physical/chemical mechanism Titan's present atmosphere — and in particular its methane component — was emplaced. Distinguish among the following hypotheses and identify which (if any) is correct:
   (i) the atmospheric methane is continuously or episodically resupplied (e.g., from a subsurface reservoir such as clathrates, a hydrocarbon-rich interior, serpentinization, or cryovolcanism), so that the steady-state CH4 inventory is geologically young;
   (ii) the present CH4 is the terminal remnant of a much larger primordial inventory that has been monotonically declining over Solar System history;
   (iii) the present CH4 was injected into the atmosphere by a relatively recent (≪ 1 Gyr) crustal-destabilization or outgassing event.

(b) Show how measurements of isotopic ratios in atmospheric and surface species (in particular D/H, 12C/13C, 14N/15N, and 36Ar/40Ar), combined with global inventories of liquid and solid hydrocarbons on the surface and in the near subsurface, can be used to place a quantitative bound on the age of the current atmosphere, and derive such a bound.

Saturn's largest moon Titan is inferred, from measurements of its degree-2 tidal Love number k_2, to host a deep liquid-water ocean beneath an outer ice shell, possibly sandwiched between an outer ice-Ih layer and a layer of high-pressure ice phases (e.g., ice VI) overlying a rocky/hydrated silicate core. The geochemical state of this internal ocean — its content of dissolved organics, salts, redox-active minerals, and biologically relevant nutrients — is poorly constrained. Determine the following:

(a) Whether Titan's deep internal water ocean is habitable, in the sense of containing concentrations of organic compounds, mineral species, and nutrients (sources of C, H, N, O, P, S and metabolically useful redox couples) sufficient to support life as currently understood, or whether it is effectively sterile.

(b) Whether each of the following exchange/transport mechanisms is physically and geochemically viable as a pathway for delivering organics, minerals, and nutrients into this ocean, and quantify the corresponding mass and chemical fluxes:
  (i) core–ocean exchange (chemical interaction between the rocky/hydrated silicate core and the overlying liquid-water ocean);
  (ii) surface–ocean exchange (transport of surface organics and other species through the outer ice shell into the ocean);
  (iii) solid-state convection within the outer ice-Ih crust;
  (iv) transport through any intervening high-pressure ice layer separating the ocean from the rocky core.

Provide criteria, in terms of interior structure, thermal state, rheology of ice phases, and chemical partitioning, under which each pathway is or is not active, and identify which combinations of active pathways suffice to sustain a habitable ocean over geologic timescales.

Consider Titan, the largest moon of Saturn, whose atmosphere supports a methane-based hydrological cycle analogous to Earth's water cycle. Methane condenses in the troposphere to form clouds, and precipitation can either reach the surface as rain or evaporate in the atmosphere as virgae before touching the ground. Surface observations reveal dry equatorial regions, a pronounced hemispheric asymmetry in the distribution of hydrocarbon lakes and seas (with most concentrated near the north pole), and geomorphological evidence of pluvial and fluvial erosion.

Determine, quantitatively:
(a) the global frequency, intensity, and spatial distribution of methane precipitation events on Titan, distinguishing between rainfall reaching the surface and virgae that evaporates aloft;
(b) how these precipitation patterns govern rates of pluvial and fluvial erosion of Titan's surface;
(c) how precipitation controls the replenishment of Titan's lakes and seas;
(d) the mechanism by which the precipitation distribution produces the observed dry equatorial belt and the north–south hemispheric asymmetry of standing liquid bodies; and
(e) the role of these processes in closing Titan's overall methane climate cycle (sources, sinks, and atmospheric/surface reservoir balance).

An Engel structure on a smooth 4-manifold $M$ is a maximally non-integrable rank-2 distribution $\mathcal{D} \subset TM$; that is, $\mathcal{E} := [\mathcal{D},\mathcal{D}]$ is a rank-3 distribution (an even-contact structure) and $[\mathcal{E},\mathcal{E}] = TM$.

Let $M$ be a smooth 4-manifold equipped with:
(i) a codimension-1 foliation $\mathcal{F}$ (so $T\mathcal{F} \subset TM$ is an integrable rank-3 distribution);
(ii) a rank-2 plane field $\xi \subset T\mathcal{F}$ which is a leafwise contact structure, i.e. for each leaf $L$ of $\mathcal{F}$, $\xi|_L$ is a contact structure on the 3-manifold $L$;
(iii) a splitting $\xi = \ell_1 \oplus \ell_2$ of $\xi$ as a direct sum of two (continuous) line fields on $M$.

Question: Does there always exist a $C^0$-small (equivalently, arbitrarily small) perturbation of the distribution $\xi$ to a rank-2 distribution $\mathcal{D} \subset TM$ that is an Engel structure on $M$?

Let $\Sigma \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ be a compact immersed surface with non-empty boundary, satisfying the following properties:

(i) $\Sigma$ has constant mean curvature;
(ii) the boundary $\partial\Sigma$ is a round circle in some plane of $\mathbb{R}^3$;
(iii) $\Sigma$ is star-shaped with respect to some point $p \in \mathbb{R}^3$, meaning that every ray emanating from $p$ meets $\Sigma$ in at most one point.

Recall that a spherical cap is the (closed) intersection of a round 2-sphere in $\mathbb{R}^3$ with a closed half-space whose bounding plane meets the sphere transversally, and a planar disk is the closed flat disk bounded by a circle in a plane.

Prove or disprove: under hypotheses (i)–(iii), $\Sigma$ must be either the flat planar disk bounded by $\partial\Sigma$ or a spherical cap with boundary $\partial\Sigma$.

Let $C \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ be a non-circular, simple closed convex planar curve lying in a plane $P$. A compact embedded constant-mean-curvature (CMC) disk $\Sigma$ spanning $C$ is a compact embedded surface in $\mathbb{R}^3$ that is topologically a disk, has constant mean curvature, and whose boundary equals $C$. For such a $\Sigma$, let $V = V(\Sigma) > 0$ denote the (signed) volume enclosed between $\Sigma$ and the planar disk bounded by $C$ in $P$.

Prove or disprove: there exists a threshold value $V_C > 0$, depending only on $C$, such that for every compact embedded CMC disk $\Sigma$ spanning $C$ with enclosed volume $V$, exactly one of the following holds:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] if $V \leq V_C$, then $\Sigma$ lies entirely in one of the two closed half-spaces determined by $P$;
\item[(ii)] if $V = V_C$, then $\Sigma$ is tangent to $P$ precisely at the points of $C$ where the planar curvature of $C$ attains its minimum;
\item[(iii)] if $V > V_C$, then $\Sigma$ contains points strictly on both sides of $P$.
\end{enumerate}

Let $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^2$ be a bounded convex domain with smooth boundary $\partial\Omega$, and let $H \in \mathbb{R}$ be a constant. Consider the constant-mean-curvature Dirichlet problem
$$\operatorname{div}\!\left(\frac{Du}{\sqrt{1+|Du|^2}}\right) = 2H \quad \text{in } \Omega, \qquad u = 0 \quad \text{on } \partial\Omega,$$
whose solutions are graphs $z = u(x,y)$ of constant mean curvature $H$ spanning $\partial\Omega$. Denote by $|\Omega|$ the area of $\Omega$, by $L$ the length of $\partial\Omega$, and by $\operatorname{diam}(\Omega)$ its Euclidean diameter. Determine whether the Dirichlet problem above admits a solution under each one of the following hypotheses separately:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $|\Omega|\,H^2 < \pi$;
\item $L\,H^2 < 2\pi$;
\item $\operatorname{diam}(\Omega) < 2/|H|$.
\end{enumerate}
That is, decide, in each of these three regimes, whether solvability of the Dirichlet problem can be guaranteed for every such bounded convex domain $\Omega$.

Background and definitions. A Polish space is a separable, completely metrizable topological space. A Polish presentation of a Polish space X is a pair (d, (x_n)_{n∈ℕ}) where d is a complete metric on X compatible with its topology and (x_n) is a dense sequence in X. The presentation is called:

• computable if the function (i, j) ↦ d(x_i, x_j) is a computable real-valued function (uniformly in i, j);
• left-c.e. if (i, j) ↦ d(x_i, x_j) is uniformly left-computable, i.e., the set {(i, j, q) ∈ ℕ × ℕ × ℚ : q < d(x_i, x_j)} is computably enumerable;
• right-c.e. if (i, j) ↦ d(x_i, x_j) is uniformly right-computable, i.e., the set {(i, j, q) ∈ ℕ × ℕ × ℚ : q > d(x_i, x_j)} is computably enumerable.

A computable Polish presentation (d, (x_n)) of a compact Polish space X is called effectively compact if there is a computable procedure that, given any natural number k, outputs a finite list of indices i_1, …, i_m such that the open balls B(x_{i_j}, 2^{-k}) cover X.

Question. Suppose X is a Polish space admitting a left-c.e. Polish presentation (d, (x_n)) under which X is compact and which is effectively compact in the above sense. Must X also admit a Polish presentation (d', (y_n)) on the same underlying set (up to homeomorphism) such that d' is a computable metric and, in addition, the metric d' is right-c.e.? Equivalently, does every effectively compact left-c.e. Polish space have a computable Polish copy whose metric is simultaneously right-c.e.?

Let $X$ be a standard Borel space and let $\vec{G} = (X, E)$ be a directed graph on $X$ whose edge set $E \subseteq X \times X$ is Borel. Assume:

(1) $\vec{G}$ is locally finite, i.e., for every vertex $x \in X$ both the in-neighborhood $\{y : (y,x) \in E\}$ and the out-neighborhood $N^+(x) = \{y : (x,y) \in E\}$ are finite;

(2) the in-degrees are uniformly bounded, i.e., there exists $d \in \mathbb{N}$ such that $|\{y : (y,x) \in E\}| \le d$ for every $x \in X$;

(3) the out-neighborhoods have polynomial growth, i.e., there exist constants $C > 0$ and $k \in \mathbb{N}$ such that for every $x \in X$ and every $n \in \mathbb{N}$, the number $|B_n^+(x)|$ of vertices reachable from $x$ by a directed path of length at most $n$ satisfies $|B_n^+(x)| \le C n^k$.

Let $E_{\vec{G}}$ be the equivalence relation on $X$ defined by $x \, E_{\vec{G}} \, y$ iff $x$ and $y$ are in the same connected component of the underlying undirected graph of $\vec{G}$. Recall that a countable Borel equivalence relation $F$ on a standard Borel space is hyperfinite if it is the union of an increasing sequence $F_0 \subseteq F_1 \subseteq \cdots$ of Borel equivalence relations all of whose classes are finite.

Question: Must $E_{\vec{G}}$ be hyperfinite?

Work with finite simple digraphs. For a digraph D, a proper k-dicolouring is a partition of V(D) into k parts each of which induces an acyclic subdigraph; the dichromatic number χ⃗(D) is the smallest such k. Two vertices u, v of a digraph are called twins if they are non-adjacent and, for every other vertex w, the arc relations between u and w match those between v and w (i.e. uw is an arc iff vw is an arc, and wu is an arc iff wv is an arc). Given two digraphs G₁ and G₂ and a set S of at most two pairwise non-adjacent vertices identified in both, the "glueing of G₁ and G₂ along S" is the digraph obtained by taking the disjoint union of G₁ and G₂ and identifying the vertices of S accordingly (with no new arcs added).

Define the class 𝒞 of digraphs recursively as the smallest class such that:
(i) the oriented K₂ (a single arc) belongs to 𝒞;
(ii) if G ∈ 𝒞 and v ∈ V(G), then the digraph obtained from G by adding a twin of v belongs to 𝒞;
(iii) if G₁, G₂ ∈ 𝒞 and S is a set of at most two pairwise non-adjacent vertices common to both, then the glueing of G₁ and G₂ along S belongs to 𝒞.

Question: Is there a constant K such that χ⃗(D) ≤ K for every D ∈ 𝒞, or does the dichromatic number of digraphs in 𝒞 take arbitrarily large values?

Provide a precise mathematical or metamathematical criterion that characterizes when a mathematical question (a formal statement in some fixed formal system, e.g. ZFC, or more generally a decision problem about a recursively enumerable set of strings) should be regarded as "interesting" or "natural." More precisely: define a formal measure I : {well-formed mathematical statements} → R (or a robustly definable subclass of statements) that captures the informal notion of being an interesting/natural question, and justify the definition by showing that:

(a) I assigns high value to a representative collection of canonical examples (e.g., the Riemann Hypothesis, P vs NP, the Continuum Hypothesis, the twin prime conjecture);
(b) I assigns low value to obviously artificial or ad hoc statements (e.g., random Gödel-style self-referential constructions, statements obtained by arbitrary padding, or instances chosen to be uninformative);
(c) I is invariant, up to a controlled error, under reasonable changes of formal language or encoding (for instance, invariant up to an additive constant under change of universal description method, in the spirit of algorithmic information theory);
(d) I is itself definable in a sufficiently expressive metatheory, and its basic properties (monotonicity, behavior under logical equivalence, behavior under conjunction/disjunction) are explicitly stated and proved.

Equivalently: formalize the predicate "Q is a natural mathematical question" in a way that is mathematically rigorous, non-trivial, and aligned with mathematical practice, or prove that no such formalization satisfying (a)–(d) can exist.

Observations of distant galaxies and quasars exhibit systematic redshifts z of their spectral features that increase with apparent distance. In the standard cosmological framework these redshifts are interpreted as a consequence of the metric expansion of the universe (with the wavelength-independent scaling λ_obs = (1+z) λ_emit and an associated time-dilation factor (1+z) for source light curves). Several alternative, non-expansion mechanisms have been proposed to account for the observed redshifts, including:

(i) gravitational redshift from compact or massive configurations along the line of sight,
(ii) "tired light" hypotheses in which photons lose energy en route through some dissipative interaction with the intergalactic medium,
(iii) variable-mass theories in which atomic emission frequencies evolve with cosmic time,
(iv) chronometric cosmology in which redshift arises from a reparametrization of time on a static background,
(v) multiple-scattering models in which redshift is induced by repeated low-angle scattering events.

Determine whether any such non-cosmological redshift mechanism can simultaneously satisfy all of the following observational constraints:
 (a) it must reproduce the observed redshift–distance relation for galaxies and quasars;
 (b) it must not produce angular blurring of distant sources beyond the levels actually observed in high-resolution imaging;
 (c) it must be achromatic to the precision with which spectra of distant sources are observed to redshift uniformly across wavelength;
 (d) it must be consistent with the (1+z) time-dilation observed in light curves and spectra of Type Ia supernovae;
 (e) it must be consistent with the precise blackbody spectrum of the cosmic microwave background and with limits on spectral distortions;
 (f) it must not violate constraints on the density, temperature, and opacity of the intergalactic medium derived from quasar absorption-line systems and other probes.

Formally: does there exist a physically consistent non-expansion redshift mechanism meeting (a)–(f), or do these observational constraints together imply that the observed redshifts require a genuinely expanding universe?

The cosmic microwave background (CMB) is observed as a nearly isotropic electromagnetic radiation field with the following properties: (i) a temperature of approximately T ≈ 2.725 K; (ii) a spectral energy distribution that is, to extremely high precision, that of a Planckian blackbody at temperature T; (iii) angular isotropy at the level of one part in 10^5 (after subtraction of the dipole); and (iv) a high degree of thermalization across the observable sky. In the standard hot Big Bang scenario, this radiation is interpreted as a relic of a hot, dense, opaque early universe that became transparent at the epoch of recombination (redshift z ≈ 1100), with thermalization achieved while photons and matter were tightly coupled.

An additional empirical constraint is that the universe is observed to be transparent to electromagnetic radiation (including radio frequencies) emitted by sources at high redshift, such as distant radio galaxies and quasars; any proposed thermalization mechanism for the CMB must be compatible with this observed transparency.

Problem. Construct, or prove the impossibility of, an alternative (non–hot Big Bang) physical scenario — for example an astrophysical mechanism (such as thermalization by intergalactic dust, plasma, or stellar relics) or a static / non-expanding cosmological model — that simultaneously:
(a) predicts a background radiation field of temperature T ≈ 2.725 K of the correct energy density;
(b) produces a spectrum that matches a Planckian blackbody to the precision currently measured;
(c) reproduces the observed large-scale isotropy;
(d) provides a quantitatively viable thermalization mechanism yielding (b) and (c); and
(e) does not require the line of sight at the relevant frequencies to be optically thick out to redshifts at which radio galaxies and other compact sources are directly observed.

Either exhibit such a model with quantitative agreement with the above constraints, or prove that conditions (a)–(e) are mutually inconsistent under stated physical assumptions.

Let the spacetime geometry of the observable universe be modelled, on sufficiently large scales, by a Friedmann–Lemaître–Robertson–Walker (FLRW) metric

  ds² = -c² dt² + a(t)² [ dr²/(1-kr²) + r²(dθ² + sin²θ dφ²) ],

which presupposes that the spatial sections are exactly homogeneous and isotropic. Let δρ(x; R) denote the fractional fluctuation of the smoothed matter density field at position x on a smoothing scale R, and let N(<R) denote the mean count of galaxies within balls of radius R centered on galaxies, with fractal/correlation dimension D₂(R) = d log N(<R) / d log R. The FLRW assumption requires the existence of a finite homogeneity scale R_H such that for all R ≥ R_H one has D₂(R) → 3 and ⟨|δρ(x;R)|²⟩^{1/2} → 0 in a statistically well-defined sense, with the distribution of galaxies and clusters approaching spatial randomness on scales R ≳ R_H.

The problem is the following: determine whether the actual large-scale matter distribution of the universe is consistent with this FLRW homogeneity-and-isotropy hypothesis, taking into account the simultaneous observational facts that

(a) coherent structures (walls, filaments, voids) appear with apparently fractal-like scaling and inhomogeneities persist at least up to scales of order 100–200 Mpc;
(b) the local Hubble law v = H₀ d is observed to be remarkably linear already in the distance range roughly 2–25 Mpc, where the matter distribution is manifestly inhomogeneous (the so-called inhomogeneity paradox);
(c) large-scale coherent peculiar velocity (bulk-flow) fields are detected on scales of tens to hundreds of Mpc;
(d) reports of quasi-periodic spatial structure ("periodic galaxy walls") and of redshift periodicities for galaxies and quasars exist;
(e) the observed near-isotropy of the cosmic microwave background, by itself, does not imply spatial homogeneity, since it is also compatible with a spherically symmetric inhomogeneous (e.g. Lemaître–Tolman–Bondi) cosmology in which the observer sits near a centre of symmetry.

Specifically: prove or disprove that there exists a finite scale R_H beyond which the observed galaxy/matter distribution is statistically homogeneous and isotropic in the sense above, so that the FLRW metric is a legitimate background description of the universe on scales R ≥ R_H.

Consider hole-doped perovskite manganites of the general form R_{1-x}A_x MnO_3, where R is a trivalent rare-earth cation (e.g., La, Pr, Nd) and A is a divalent alkaline-earth cation (e.g., Ca, Sr, Ba), and x denotes the hole concentration. Such compounds exhibit colossal magnetoresistance and, depending on x and temperature, ground states ranging from antiferromagnetic charge/orbital-ordered insulators to ferromagnetic metals, with widespread theoretical and experimental indications of intrinsic electronic inhomogeneity ("phase separation") into coexisting nanoscale domains of competing phases.

Carry out atomic-resolution scanning tunneling microscopy (STM) and scanning tunneling spectroscopy (STS) measurements on cleaved single-crystal or epitaxial-film surfaces of such manganites across a broad range of hole densities x, with particular emphasis on compositions whose low-temperature ground state is a ferromagnetic metal. Determine, as functions of x, temperature, and applied magnetic field:

(a) the existence, spatial morphology, length scales, and volume fractions of coexisting electronic phases (e.g., metallic versus insulating, charge-ordered versus uniform) at the atomic and nanometer scales;
(b) the local density of states and local single-particle gap (or pseudogap) within each phase, including the spatial profile across phase boundaries;
(c) the correlation of these local electronic features with the underlying lattice and any observed charge/orbital ordering patterns;
(d) how the inhomogeneous electronic landscape evolves through the ferromagnetic-metal/insulator transition and across the CMR regime.

Provide a coherent description of phase separation and local electronic structure in manganites obtained from these atomic-resolution STM/STS data over the full hole-doping range, including the ferromagnetic-metallic compositions.

Setup. Spin-density-functional theory (SDFT) extends Kohn–Sham density-functional theory by treating, in addition to the electron density n(r), the vector spin magnetization m(r) = (m_x(r), m_y(r), m_z(r)) as a fundamental variable. The ground-state energy is written as a functional E[n, m] = T_s[n,m] + ∫ v_ext(r) n(r) dr − ∫ B_ext(r)·m(r) dr + E_H[n] + E_xc[n, m], with T_s the noninteracting kinetic energy, v_ext and B_ext external scalar and magnetic potentials, E_H the Hartree energy, and E_xc the exchange–correlation (xc) functional. Variation with respect to the 2×2 spin-density matrix yields Kohn–Sham equations with an effective scalar potential v_s(r) and an effective magnetic field B_s(r) = B_ext(r) + B_xc(r), where the xc magnetic field is B_xc(r) := −δE_xc[n,m]/δm(r).

A spin configuration is called noncollinear if m(r) is not everywhere parallel to a single fixed axis. In the local-spin-density approximation (LSDA), one takes E_xc^{LSDA}[n,m] = ∫ ε_xc^{hom}(n(r), |m(r)|) dr, which forces B_xc(r) ∥ m(r) pointwise. Standard noncollinear SDFT implementations impose this parallelity at every point, so that the local xc torque m(r) × B_xc(r) vanishes. A generalized-gradient approximation (GGA) takes the form E_xc^{GGA}[n,m] = ∫ f(n, m, ∇n, ∇m) dr; in general such functionals produce B_xc(r) that is not parallel to m(r), so the parallelity constraint is inconsistent with GGA. As an alternative, one may reformulate SDFT in terms of the off-diagonal element (the "staggered density") of the one-body spin-density matrix, but this requires constructing new approximations of E_xc as a functional of that variable and solving a more complicated Kohn–Sham system.

Problem. Provide a theoretically consistent and computationally practical formulation of noncollinear spin-density-functional theory beyond LSDA. Specifically, identify a class of xc functionals E_xc[n,m] (or a reformulation in terms of an equivalent set of variables such as the staggered density) together with associated Kohn–Sham equations that simultaneously satisfies all of the following requirements:
(i) reduces to the standard collinear SDFT when m(r) is everywhere parallel to a fixed axis;
(ii) is rotationally invariant in spin space (and, in the presence of spin–orbit coupling, jointly under spin–space rotations consistent with the underlying Hamiltonian);
(iii) is compatible with gradient-dependent (GGA-type) approximations without imposing the unphysical pointwise constraint B_xc(r) ∥ m(r);
(iv) yields physically correct local xc torques m(r) × B_xc(r) and the correct global zero-torque sum rule ∫ m(r) × B_xc(r) dr = 0;
(v) is numerically stable, in particular in regions where |m(r)| is small or vanishes;
(vi) is computationally tractable for realistic inhomogeneous many-electron systems.

Determine the optimal such formulation, or prove that no formulation can satisfy all of (i)–(vi) simultaneously.

Consider the classical maximum-entropy (MaxEnt) inference framework based on the Shannon–Jaynes Principle of Indifference: given a probability distribution p over a measurable state space, one assigns p by maximizing the Shannon entropy S[p] = -∫ p(x) log p(x) dx (or, relative to a prior q, minimizes the Kullback–Leibler cross-entropy D(p‖q) = ∫ p(x) log(p(x)/q(x)) dx) subject to a finite set of expectation-value constraints. This construction yields exponential-family distributions and, in physical applications, leads to standard Fickian/Fourier diffusion equations and additive (Euler homogeneous of order one) thermodynamic potentials.

There are, however, nonequilibrium phenomena — including anomalous (Lévy-type) diffusion, fractal Brownian motion, and self-organized criticality — whose stationary or transient statistics are governed by heavy-tailed, non-exponential distributions for which additivity of the entropy fails and the standard Fick/Fourier diffusion equations are inadequate.

Problem. Formulate precise mathematical criteria (on the underlying dynamics, the constraint algebra, or the geometry of the admissible probability simplex) characterizing exactly those situations in which the Shannon–Jaynes Principle of Indifference fails to produce the physically correct probability assignment. In each such regime, identify a generalized entropy functional S_g[p] and an associated cross-entropy (relative information) functional D_g(p‖q) that:

(i) reduce to the Shannon and Kullback–Leibler functionals in an appropriate limit;
(ii) when maximized/minimized under physically natural constraints, yield the observed non-exponential (e.g. Lévy-stable, q-exponential, stretched exponential, or power-law) distributions;
(iii) generate evolution equations consistent with anomalous diffusion, fractal kinetics, and scale-invariant critical statistics;
(iv) satisfy a coherent set of information-theoretic axioms (positivity, concavity, a suitably weakened additivity/composability rule, and a data-processing inequality).

Determine whether a single such generalized entropy suffices universally, or whether a family of inequivalent entropies (parameterized by the physical context) is necessary, and characterize this family.

Polchinski's inductive scheme for perturbative renormalization proceeds as follows: one introduces a smooth ultraviolet cutoff in the propagator of a Euclidean quantum field theory, writes down a first-order differential equation (the Polchinski flow equation) for the scale-dependent effective action obtained by integrating out high momenta, and proves perturbative renormalizability by an inductive bound on the scale derivatives of the connected amputated Green functions, organized by the canonical scaling dimension of each interaction vertex. The argument is purely inductive in the renormalization-group scale, avoids explicit Feynman-graph combinatorics, and treats relevant and marginal couplings by integrating the flow equation from the renormalization scale, irrelevant ones from the ultraviolet scale.

Problem. Devise a non-perturbative (constructive) renormalization scheme for Euclidean quantum field theories that
 (i) is fully rigorous, i.e. produces convergent (not merely formal) bounds on the effective action and its functional derivatives uniform in the ultraviolet cutoff, and that yields the Borel summability or analyticity of the Schwinger functions in the coupling constant in a suitable domain;
 (ii) is organized as a single inductive argument on the renormalization-group scale, of comparable conceptual and technical simplicity to Polchinski's perturbative induction (in particular, not relying on cluster or multiscale expansions with explicit tree/forest formulas, large/small field decompositions, or domination procedures specific to each model);
 (iii) applies to fermionic models, where the effective action is a formal power series in anticommuting Grassmann variables and the natural Gaussian "probability" measure is not positive, in such a way that the inductive bounds are obtained directly at the level of the flow equation for the Grassmann effective action.

Either construct such a scheme, or prove that no scheme satisfying (i)–(iii) can exist within a precisely specified framework.

In the framework of constructive (Euclidean) quantum field theory, many models of interest possess important symmetries — for instance, local gauge symmetry (invariance under a compact or non-compact Lie group acting fiberwise on the fields), supersymmetry (a graded symmetry mixing bosonic and fermionic field components, often used to represent determinants or to implement disorder averaging), and replica symmetry (the permutation symmetry S_n acting on n identical copies of a system, used in the replica method for quenched disorder and typically requiring an n → 0 limit). Constructive analysis of such theories generally requires either (i) a rigorous procedure for quotienting out the symmetry (e.g., gauge fixing without resorting to perturbative Faddeev–Popov manipulations, or taking rigorous replica limits) or (ii) a controlled mechanism for symmetry breaking, in a way compatible with non-perturbative bounds (cluster or multiscale expansions, Borel summability, etc.). Moreover, the formal Ward identities expressing infinitesimal invariance of correlation functions have, in most non-trivial models, only been established at the level of formal power series.

Problem. Develop general non-perturbative methods, applicable within constructive field theory, to:

(a) rigorously quotient out gauge symmetry, supersymmetry, and replica symmetry in interacting Euclidean field theory models, producing well-defined measures (or correlation functions) on the quotient space and uniform non-perturbative estimates (e.g., stability bounds, convergent expansions, Borel summability) that survive the quotient/limit;

(b) rigorously implement controlled breakings of such symmetries (gauge fixing, soft supersymmetry breaking, finite-n replica setups followed by analytic continuation to n → 0) with explicit non-perturbative error control;

(c) establish a general theory of non-perturbative Ward identities — that is, exact identities among correlation functions of the constructed (non-perturbative) measure expressing the infinitesimal symmetries — together with criteria guaranteeing their validity in concrete constructive models (gauge theories, supersymmetric models, replica models of disordered systems).

Provide such methods at a level of generality that goes beyond model-by-model arguments, and apply them to representative examples (e.g., a non-Abelian lattice or continuum gauge theory, a supersymmetric matrix or sigma model, or a replica formulation of a disordered system).
